Example target: https://target.example/graphql
Vulnerable resolver (pseudo‑code):
function setLink(context, args) {
  // args.id is taken from the client
  const link = db.findLinkById(args.id); // no ownership check
  link.title = args.title;
  link.url = args.url;
  db.save(link);
}
Attack request (JSON body):
{
  "query": "mutation { setDigitalDownloadLinkContext(id: \"12345\", title: \"Hacked\", url: \"https://evil.example\") { id title url } }"
}
Why it works: The server trusts the supplied id ("12345") and updates the record even though the attacker does not own it, because there is no check that the authenticated user’s account matches the link’s owner.

lesson.fix:
Add an explicit authorization check in every resolver that receives a Link id. Pseudo‑code fix:
function setLink(context, args) {
  const link = db.findLinkById(args.id);
  if (link.ownerWorkspaceId !== context.authenticatedUser.workspaceId) {
    throw new Error('Unauthorized');
  }
  // proceed with update
  link.title = args.title;
  link.url = args.url;
  db.save(link);
}
The check ensures the link belongs to the same workspace as the authenticated user before any modification. Apply this pattern to all GraphQL mutations and queries that accept object identifiers.

lesson.example:
Imagine a fictional site called 'DigitalArtStore.example'. When you click on a painting to see its price, the website sends a request to get the painting's details. 

Vulnerable Code (Server-side):
function getProductDetails(productId) {
  const product = db.find(productId);
  // VULNERABILITY: Generating the download link for EVERYONE who views the product page
  const downloadUrl = s3.generatePresignedUrl(product.filePath, { expires: 3600 });
  return { name: product.name, price: product.price, url: downloadUrl };
}

Attack Request:
POST /graphql
Content: { "query": "{ product(id: 50) { name price url } }" }

Response:
{ "data": { "product": { "name": "Sunset.jpg", "price": "$50.00", "url": "https://s3.example/Sunset.jpg?sig=abc123..." } } }

Why it works: The server generates the 'url' (the secret download link) and sends it to the user's browser before checking if the user has actually paid the $50.00. The attacker simply copies the 'url' from the background data and downloads the image for free.

